+## Docker settings (daemon.json and defaults)
+
+**Docker → Settings** (`/docker/settings`, 0.134.0+;
+[`app/services/docker_daemon.py`](https://github.com/sparkycz1/debcontrol/blob/main/app/services/docker_daemon.py))
+keeps the Docker Engine set up the same way on every machine - or only
+on the machine groups ticked under *Machines* - and sets the defaults
+for containers debcontrol sees for the first time.
+
+**The `daemon.json` keys.** Each field left on *Keep as it is* (or
+empty) is not managed: debcontrol changes only the keys set here and
+keeps everything else in `/etc/docker/daemon.json` as it is.
+
+| Setting | `daemon.json` | Notes |
+|---|---|---|
+| Log driver | `log-driver` | `json-file` (Docker's default), `local` (compressed, recommended), `journald` |
+| Maximum size of one file / Files kept | `log-opts.max-size` / `max-file` | e.g. `20m` and `5`: at most 100 MB per container; only for `json-file` and `local` |
+| Live restore | `live-restore` | containers keep running while the daemon restarts or is upgraded (not with Swarm) |
+| No new privileges | `no-new-privileges` | processes in containers can't gain rights (setuid, sudo) |
+| Build cache kept | `builder.gc.defaultKeepStorage` (and `enabled`) | e.g. `20GB` |
+| Address pools for new networks | `default-address-pools` | one per line, `10.210.0.0/16 24`: a private IPv4 range and network size (prefix to /29), at most 10 - keeps Docker off your LAN or VPN addresses |
+| DNS servers for containers | `dns` | up to 4 IP addresses |
+| Docker Hub mirrors | `registry-mirrors` | up to 5 `http(s)://` addresses without a user name or password |
+
+Docker reads most of these only when it restarts, and the log settings
+and address pools apply to containers and networks created afterwards.
+
+**Writing and restarting are two steps.** *Save* only stores the
+settings (`settings.manage`; not for an account limited to some machine
+groups, since they are for the whole fleet). Under *Where the machines
+stand* every machine in scope shows whether it matches (from its last
+[host check](#the-docker-host-check)), what differs, and whether a
+restart is waiting:
+
+- **Write to machines (N)** (`settings.manage` + `action.terminal`; also
+  *Write daemon.json* on one machine's Host page, `action.terminal`)
+  queues a *Write daemon.json* job per machine. It reads the current
+  file (an unreadable or invalid one is never written over), merges the
+  managed keys in, and - when something changes - checks the new file
+  with `dockerd --validate` (Docker 23+; older says it couldn't), copies
+  the old one to `daemon.json.debcontrol.bak` and installs the new one
+  as root (`sudo -n`; a no-op for root). The file goes over standard
+  input, never into a command line. Docker is not restarted; the machine
+  shows *Restart needed*.
+- **Restart Docker where waiting (N)** (`action.power`; also *Restart
+  Docker* on one machine's Host page) queues a *Docker restart* job per
+  such machine: `systemctl restart docker` (or `service`), then waits up
+  to 90 s for `docker info`. If Docker doesn't come back, the backup is
+  put back and Docker restarted again, and the job fails saying so.
+  Containers without live restore stop for a moment and start again per
+  their restart policy. **The machine debcontrol runs on is never
+  restarted this way** - restart Docker there yourself
+  (`sudo systemctl restart docker`) when convenient.
+
+**Defaults for new containers.** *Automatic updates* (off, or patch /
+minor / any), *Vulnerability gate* and *Autoheal for containers with a
+health check*. A container gets them the first time a monitoring sample
+sees it (never one that existed before, never debcontrol's own, and
+autoheal only when it has a health check); a container's own settings,
+once changed, always win. A new stack's form starts with the default
+gate.
+
+Audited as `docker.settings.update` (what changed), `docker.settings.apply`
+(how many machines) and per machine `machine.docker.daemon.apply` /
+`machine.docker.daemon.restart`.
+
